    {
      "sectionId": "event-loop",
      "title": "What is Event Loop in Node.js?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "isHtml": true,
          "text": "The <strong>Event Loop</strong> is the core mechanism of Node.js that allows asynchronous execution. Instead of waiting for tasks like file reading or database queries to finish, Node.js continues executing other tasks."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "isHtml": true,
          "text": "This non-blocking architecture makes Node.js extremely fast and scalable for real-time applications."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "sectionId": "npm-section",
      "title": "What is npm?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "isHtml": true,
          "text": "<strong>npm (Node Package Manager)</strong> is the default package manager for Node.js. It provides access to millions of open-source packages."
        },
        {
          "type": "codeBox",
          "lang": "🟢 Terminal",
          "label": "npm commands",
          "lines": [
            "npm init -y",
            "npm install express",
            "npm install mongoose",
            "npm start"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "sectionId": "modules-nodejs",
      "title": "Node.js Modules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Modules are reusable blocks of code in Node.js. Node.js supports both built-in modules and custom modules."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparisonTable",
          "headers": ["Module", "Purpose"],
          "rows": [
            ["fs", "File system operations"],
